Project: Linux Kernel Character Device Driver + User-space Controller
Project summary (one-line for resume)

“Implemented a Linux kernel character device driver for an ultrasonic distance sensor with a user-space daemon that reads sensor data and publishes to MQTT. Demonstrates kernel-level device interfacing, interrupt-safe design, and real-time data bridging to IoT.”

Architecture (high level)
[HC-SR04 Sensor] --GPIO--> [Linux Kernel Module (char device)] <---> /dev/sensorX
                                                         |
                                                         | read()/ioctl from user-space
                                                         v
                                             [User-space daemon (C)] --MQTT--> [Broker/Node-RED]
                                                         |
                                                         v
                                           Dashboard / Logging / Alerting


Modules

Kernel module (C):

Expose /dev/ultrasonic0 character device

Configure GPIOs, use kernel timer or interrupt (if hardware supports) to trigger & read echo

Provide read() which returns last measured distance (atomic snapshot)

Provide ioctl to change sampling period, threshold, or start/stop

Use workqueue / tasklet or kernel thread to perform measurements (keep ISR minimal)

User-space daemon (C):

Opens /dev/ultrasonic0, performs blocking reads (or polls with select)

Publishes readings to MQTT (calls system() to mosquitto_pub or links libmosquitto)

Optionally serves an HTTP/Socket interface for dashboard

Hardware & Platform

Recommended: Raspberry Pi (Linux) — you can use GPIO and test easily.

Sensor: HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor (Trig and Echo lines). Use appropriate level shifting if needed.

Safety & notes

Kernel code must avoid long blocking or heavy work inside IRQ context. Use workqueues or kernel threads.

Measuring HC-SR04 echo timing in kernel is possible via ktime_get() and busy-waiting with short sleeps (or use high-res timers). For a demo it's acceptable; for production, use hardware timers or user-space realtime.

